        </div>
        <p>As you can see there are two possible perpendiculars to the plane, one is <em>up</em> and another is <em>down</em>. Which one should we take as the direction of the plane? The choice is arbitrary. There is no reason to prefer one above the other. But a choice has to be made. First draw a <em>right-handed</em> co-ordinate system in the plane. Face the {`\\(y\\)`}-axis such that the {`\\(x\\)`}-axis is to your right. Now look <em>up</em>. This <em>up</em>-direction is fixed as the direction of the plane.</p>
    </section>
    <section>
        <h2>Cross product</h2>
        <p>
            The <em>cross</em> product or <em>vector</em> product of two vectors {`\\(\\vec{a}\\)`} and {`\\(\\vec{b}\\)`} is defined as {`\\[\\vec{a}\\times\\vec{b} = ab\\sin\\theta\\hat{n}\\]`} where {`\\(\\theta\\)`} is the angle between {`\\(\\vec{a}\\)`} and {`\\(\\vec{b}\\)`} and {`\\(\\hat{n}\\)`} is a unit vector perpendicular to the plane containing {`\\(\\vec{a}\\)`} and {`\\(\\vec{b}\\)`} as defined above. Notice that there are two angles between any two intersecting lines. One is is less than {`\\(180^\\circ\\)`} and another is more than {`\\(180^\\circ\\)`}. The angle {`\\(\\theta\\)`} is the smaller of the two values. As for the direction of {`\\(\\hat{n}\\)`}, imagine the vectors {`\\(\\vec{a}\\)`} and {`\\(\\vec{b}\\)`} are rotated together such that {`\\(\\vec{b}\\)`} points along the {`\\(y\\)`}-axis, then the direction that makes {`\\(\\vec{a}\\)`} to your right will be the direction of {`\\(\\hat{n}\\)`}. 
        </p>
