    <ul>
        <li>The cross product is maximum when {`\\(\\theta = 90^\\circ\\)`} with value {`\\(\\vec{a}\\times\\vec{b} = ab\\)`}.</li>
        <li>The cross product is minimum when {`\\(\\theta = -90^\\circ\\)`} with value {`\\(\\vec{a}\\times\\vec{b} = -ab\\)`}.</li>
        <li>The cross product is zero when {`\\(\\theta = 0^\\circ\\)`} with value {`\\(\\vec{a}\\times\\vec{b} = \\vec{0}\\)`}.</li>
        <li>The cross product is <strong>not</strong> commutative, i.e. {`\\(\\vec{a}\\times\\vec{b} = -\\vec{b}\\times\\vec{a}\\)`}.</li>
        <li>The cross product is distributive, i.e. {`\\(\\vec{a}\\times(\\vec{b} + \\vec{c}) = \\vec{a}\\times\\vec{b} + \\vec{a}\\times\\vec{c}\\)`} </li>
        <li>The cross product is <strong>not</strong> associative, i.e. {`\\(\\vec{a}\\times(\\vec{b} \\times \\vec{c}) \\neq (\\vec{a}\\times\\vec{b}) \\times\\vec{c}\\)`} </li>
    </ul>
    <section>
        <h2>Unit vectors</h2>
        <p>The cross product of unit vectors {`\\(\\hat{i}, \\hat{j}, \\hat{k}\\)`} will be {`
        \\[
            \\begin{eqnarray}
                \\hat{i}\\times\\hat{i} = \\hat{j}\\times\\hat{j} = \\hat{k}\\times\\hat{k} = 0\\\\
                \\hat{i}\\times\\hat{j} = \\hat{k},~ \\hat{j}\\times\\hat{k} = \\hat{i}, ~\\hat{k}\\times\\hat{i} &=& j\\\\
                \\hat{j}\\times\\hat{i} = -\\hat{k},~ \\hat{k}\\times\\hat{j} = -\\hat{i}, ~\\hat{i}\\times\\hat{k} &=& -j
            \\end{eqnarray}
            \\]`}
            The following triangle can be used to remember these relations: follow in the direction to get positive values and follow in the opposite direction to get negative values. For example, {`\\(\\hat{i}\\times\\hat{j}\\)`} means move from {`\\(\\hat{i}\\)`} to {`\\(\\hat{j}\\)`}. This is along the arrows. Hence the result is positive {`\\(\\hat{k}\\)`}. On the other hand, {`\\(\\hat{j}\\times\\hat{i}\\)`} means move from {`\\(\\hat{j}\\)`} to {`\\(\\hat{i}\\)`}. This is opposite to the arrows. Hence the result is negative {`\\(\\hat{k}\\)`}, i.e. means move from {`\\(\\hat{i}\\)`} to {`\\(\\hat{j}\\)`}. This is along the arrows. Hence the result is positive {`\\(-\\hat{k}\\)`}.
        </p>
